            "text_89QDy6": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>Many women find their sleep changes during perimenopause. Night sweats can wake you repeatedly, and falling progesterone, which has a calming effect, may make it harder to fall and stay asleep. Anxiety, needing the bathroom at night, and aches and pains can all play a part.</p><p>The risk of sleep apnea, where breathing repeatedly pauses during sleep, also rises after menopause. Poor sleep then makes other symptoms, from brain fog to low mood, feel worse, so improving it often has knock-on benefits.</p>",
                "text_style": "body",
                "alignment": "left",
                "text_color": "",
                "custom_font_size": false,
                "font_size": 16,
                "font_size_mobile": 15,
                "custom_line_height": false,
